 Good structural bones already (honest local-only architecture, accessibility basics, two clear tabs). Here's what's actually missing for taking this to market, roughly
  in priority order:

  Data safety — the biggest risk
  Everything lives in localStorage only. No export/import. One cleared cache or browser reinstall and a user's custom chains, streak history, and session log are gone
  permanently. Before you put this in front of real users, I'd add a simple "export/import as JSON" — cheap to build, protects people from losing work they'll come to
  care about.

  Trust surface
  No Privacy Policy or About page anywhere. Even though the whole pitch is "nothing leaves your browser," people won't take that on faith without it being stated
  somewhere explicit — and it matters more once this isn't just you using it.

  Settings
  Zero preferences screen. No way to mute the chime, see/clear what's stored, or understand the no-account model. Doesn't need to be elaborate, but a single
  About/Settings page covers three gaps at once.

  Missing basics
  No favicon or app icons at all right now — index.html doesn't reference one.

  Reliability
  The countdown itself is actually solid — it recomputes from wall-clock time (endTime), so it self-corrects even if the tab is throttled in the background. But there's
  no notification path: if the user isn't looking at the tab when a session ends, there's nothing to alert them beyond the in-tab chime.

  PWA readiness
  No manifest.json or service worker. Adding these gets you installable/offline-capable on desktop and Android for free, and is a real stepping stone toward what you're
  describing for iOS.

  On the App Store plan specifically — worth knowing now: Apple's review guideline 4.2 rejects apps that are just a website wrapped in a WebView with no native
  functionality, so "package the site as-is" likely won't pass review. The bigger issue is functional, not just political: a backgrounded iOS app can't run JS timers, so
  if someone starts a 25-minute session and switches apps, nothing can alert them when it ends without a scheduled local notification — which requires a real native
  layer (something like Capacitor with a notifications plugin, or a native rewrite of just that piece). Good to keep in mind as you build, so you're not assuming the web
  app becomes the app store app unchanged.

